This is Hacker Public Radio Episode 4746 from onday 12th October 2026.

00:00:12.710 --> 00:00:15.710
Today's show is entitled, Monotone.

00:00:15.710 --> 00:00:19.710
It is the 40th show of Lee, and is about 12 minutes long.

00:00:19.710 --> 00:00:21.710
It carries a clean flag.

00:00:21.710 --> 00:00:25.710
The summary is, lead us some audio and textual analysis in Python.

00:00:25.710 --> 00:00:41.890
Hi, I'm Lee.

00:00:41.890 --> 00:00:44.890
Today I'm going to start talking about some satire,

00:00:44.890 --> 00:00:48.890
then move on to some computer-based audio and semantic analysis.

00:00:48.890 --> 00:00:53.750
and finally tried to draw some sort of conclusion linking the two.

00:00:53.750 --> 00:00:59.270
End-gate.com is a satirical tech blog that basically reads what the Hacker News website

00:00:59.270 --> 00:01:05.990
is posting, then reposts it with a sarcastic comment. In July I saw a repost on Mastodon

00:01:05.990 --> 00:01:11.550
mentioning Hacker Public Radio. Apparently HBR has been mentioned on Hacker News and therefore

00:01:11.550 --> 00:01:18.310
fell victim to the end-gated treatment. There were two main assertions. First the HPR

00:01:18.310 --> 00:01:25.470
is hosted by some in quotes, tech wizards with emojis, and I'll leave that one alone

00:01:25.470 --> 00:01:30.670
as it's clearly true considering contributors like my dear old mum where she talked about

00:01:30.670 --> 00:01:36.730
being a Telax operator in the 1970s and my blue-haired Dutch friend, Beno, who goes around

00:01:36.730 --> 00:01:42.390
cycling and playing Pokemon Go on this iPhone. The second assertion was that the content

00:01:42.390 --> 00:01:47.590
and delivery is extremely modern-time, while the mix of shows is incoherent in quotes

00:01:47.590 --> 00:01:53.590
a cacophony, and finally the dearth of eager new contributors was mentioned. As that's

00:01:53.590 --> 00:01:58.390
old territory from previous discussions on the topic, I also leave that subject be for

00:01:58.390 --> 00:02:04.950
now. What was most interesting to me was the assertion of monotonicity, since it was referred

00:02:04.950 --> 00:02:10.350
to more than once in the tweet. This certainly sounded like something worth investigating,

00:02:10.350 --> 00:02:15.710
as to me this sounded like an accurate impression, but I couldn't now down it's exact veracity.

00:02:15.710 --> 00:02:19.030
But how would we test this impression?

00:02:19.030 --> 00:02:24.070
As it turned out, monotone is a slippery word, as it can refer to both the sound of someone's

00:02:24.070 --> 00:02:29.830
speech, as well as a range of expression, both in sentiment and semantics.

00:02:29.830 --> 00:02:33.550
For me, the easiest area to tackle first was sound, though.

00:02:33.550 --> 00:02:37.910
As you probably are well aware, sound is a wive that travels through the air and consists

00:02:37.910 --> 00:02:40.350
of a mixture of different frequencies.

00:02:40.350 --> 00:02:46.230
At any instant, we can both perceive whether the pitch of someone's voice is high or low,

00:02:46.230 --> 00:02:48.910
as well as how much variation there is.

00:02:48.910 --> 00:02:52.410
It turns out this is also something a computer can measure.

00:02:52.410 --> 00:02:56.870
If the variation in pitch is scanned, then we call that speech monotone.

00:02:56.870 --> 00:02:59.750
Okay, so how would I begin?

00:02:59.750 --> 00:03:04.410
I couldn't just examine Hackapublic radio, episode audio, and isolation, as that would

00:03:04.410 --> 00:03:06.270
give me no point of comparison.

00:03:06.270 --> 00:03:11.950
Luckily, I had handy downloads from other podcasts, Darknet Diaries, Destination Linux,

00:03:11.950 --> 00:03:14.830
the Bugcast, Linux Matters and Smothers.

00:03:14.830 --> 00:03:19.630
My first task was choosing what episodes or sections of episodes to compare.

00:03:19.630 --> 00:03:23.630
Rather than analyse every single episode, which could be hundreds of gigabytes of audio

00:03:23.630 --> 00:03:28.030
data, it was more practical to systematically select a random sample.

00:03:28.030 --> 00:03:32.750
Also, to keep this grounded in more or less recent times, for what I think was the longest

00:03:32.750 --> 00:03:39.310
running podcast APR, I limited the research to just episodes later than 3000.

00:03:39.310 --> 00:03:44.270
Then rather than just feeding these episodes into an audio analyzer, I needed to be selective

00:03:44.270 --> 00:03:49.510
so as not to include stuff that's not talking like static noise and music. Thankfully, a

00:03:49.510 --> 00:03:54.870
whole Linux to noise ratio can be calculated for audio samples. And this metric let me filter

00:03:54.870 --> 00:04:00.230
out parts of just noise. Then the music detector algorithm could be used to filter out jingles

00:04:00.230 --> 00:04:06.710
and such. To help get just speech I could also apply a technical voice activity detection,

00:04:06.710 --> 00:04:10.310
which triggers when it hears talking versus any other kind of sound.

00:04:10.310 --> 00:04:15.870
Now the frequency data I was getting had to be converted from hertz, which varies logarithmically

00:04:15.870 --> 00:04:22.430
with pitch to semitones, which change linearly with pitch. Thus we could get a real meaningful

00:04:22.430 --> 00:04:27.830
and fair measurement of how audibly monotone the speech was. For the speech clip sampled

00:04:27.830 --> 00:04:34.370
in every episode a medium pitch was derived as well as a statistical measure of the variation

00:04:34.370 --> 00:04:39.710
in the pitch. This technique allowed a range of different people to be fairly compared,

00:04:39.710 --> 00:04:43.750
whatever their baseline pitch was, which for a mouse beaker for example you might expect

00:04:43.750 --> 00:04:49.510
to be lower. And guess what, the raw data from this experiment does bear out the initial

00:04:49.510 --> 00:04:55.990
assertion, speech in HP and I tend to be significantly more one-a-tane. But finding a statistical

00:04:55.990 --> 00:04:59.350
differences one thing. Understanding why it was there is another.

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:03.240
I considered several explanations.

00:05:03.240 --> 00:05:07.160
First what came to mind was that tech hobbyist communities might have a higher prevalence of

00:05:07.160 --> 00:05:12.720
neurodivergent contributors, but we might imagine have less expressive speech.

00:05:12.720 --> 00:05:17.600
Well this did not bear out, literature on the subject, if anything suggests autistic people

00:05:17.600 --> 00:05:20.960
can on the whole have a wider pitch range in their speech.

00:05:20.960 --> 00:05:22.800
This actually surprised me.

00:05:22.800 --> 00:05:25.440
They're not considered whether acts are played apart.

00:05:25.440 --> 00:05:27.960
The difference between American or British contributors.

00:05:27.960 --> 00:05:31.560
Why, anecdotally, that seemed a plausible factor.

00:05:31.560 --> 00:05:35.680
I could not find much real evidence about this.

00:05:35.680 --> 00:05:40.320
Then also another factor that there are contributors using English as a second language.

00:05:40.320 --> 00:05:44.280
Actually, this is plausible since there is evidence that there is generally less pitch

00:05:44.280 --> 00:05:46.520
variation in that case.

00:05:46.520 --> 00:05:51.320
But this scenario does not seem to account for the majority of shows.

00:05:51.320 --> 00:05:55.120
Other demographic factors such as age and gender will possible candidates that might

00:05:55.120 --> 00:06:00.480
contribute in some way to the observed result. And while yes I considered these factors,

00:06:00.480 --> 00:06:05.200
I am clear in my own mind that it's totally okay for different communities to have different

00:06:05.200 --> 00:06:10.720
demographics. Understanding why something is observed is not the same as saying what should

00:06:10.720 --> 00:06:16.400
be observed. But after considering all those things, the simplest explanation seemed not

00:06:16.400 --> 00:06:22.640
to be who was recording but how the podcast was recorded. Research on speech shows that a

00:06:22.640 --> 00:06:28.000
a person reading from notes naturally has a flat pitch pattern than two or more people

00:06:28.000 --> 00:06:31.880
having a live conversation. And we would indeed know that while there are interviews

00:06:31.880 --> 00:06:36.840
and conversations in HPR, there are also a lot of episodes like this one with one person

00:06:36.840 --> 00:06:42.120
expanding on their chosen topic. This contrasted to the comparison podcast that were often

00:06:42.120 --> 00:06:47.800
consistently to or more hosts speaking. I did go down the rabbit hole trying to prove

00:06:47.800 --> 00:06:52.840
how many hosts per HPI episode there was, without having to listen to everyone in the

00:06:52.840 --> 00:06:58.080
sample. First I tried scanning metadata and transcripts, then even applying clustering

00:06:58.080 --> 00:07:03.640
techniques to the sound. What I did discover was that even solo shows often appear to have

00:07:03.640 --> 00:07:07.880
more than one contributor because noise and other artifacts were being picked up as a

00:07:07.880 --> 00:07:12.640
distinct audio source. In the end I gave this up and just accepted what I knew in the first

00:07:12.640 --> 00:07:18.160
place a lot of shows are silo recordings. In any case this had answered any part of my

00:07:18.160 --> 00:07:24.480
original question, yes the audio is more one-at-one, but what about the words and ideas themselves?

00:07:24.480 --> 00:07:29.400
How could I tell if these dominant leads had just a single, subject and flat sentiment,

00:07:29.400 --> 00:07:32.400
whether they represented a range of themes and emotions?

00:07:32.400 --> 00:07:38.100
Well to start with I needed textual transcripts, thankfully the website already provided fairly

00:07:38.100 --> 00:07:42.900
good ones, but just in case I needed to derive my own, a tool like Whisper could do a reasonable

00:07:42.900 --> 00:07:48.500
job of converting speech to text. I'll qualify this by saying the quality of transcription

00:07:48.500 --> 00:07:53.820
is dependent on the source audio, and even then, proper nouns especially often be misheard.

00:07:53.820 --> 00:07:58.740
But once I had the text I saw there were actually a lot of different properties and

00:07:58.740 --> 00:08:04.300
metrics that could be potentially applied. One of them is wording beddings, which are

00:08:04.300 --> 00:08:09.380
often used in AI and can represent words in the multi-dimensional vector space, where similar

00:08:09.380 --> 00:08:14.060
meaning words are close to each other. So with this technique, how scared the words were

00:08:14.060 --> 00:08:19.140
in this space would indicate what range of different meanings were contained. There was

00:08:19.140 --> 00:08:24.780
also variation in sentence length, variation in grammar and sentence structure, there's

00:08:24.780 --> 00:08:31.300
emotional tone that can be measured with sentiment scoring algorithms, there's how often a narrative

00:08:31.300 --> 00:08:36.420
uses words that pivot in a different direction like however or on the other hand.

00:08:36.420 --> 00:08:42.180
How often does the person ask questions whether rhetorical or to another speaker?

00:08:42.180 --> 00:08:46.300
There's also how compressible the text is. Text with lots of structural patterns that

00:08:46.300 --> 00:08:53.740
compress as well is seen as containing less original and unique ideas and linguistic variety.

00:08:53.740 --> 00:08:58.020
So for reference material to be used as a point of comparison, I was best not to use

00:08:58.020 --> 00:09:03.100
a large body of multi-host shows I had before. Also I didn't necessarily have transcripts for

00:09:03.100 --> 00:09:08.220
those. Instead I used TED Talk which are publicly available and do have transcripts.

00:09:08.220 --> 00:09:13.180
So after applying all this analysis the results were mixed. So it turned out, HPR episodes

00:09:13.180 --> 00:09:18.900
did register as having less emotional swing and more repeated vocab and phrasing. On the

00:09:18.900 --> 00:09:23.540
other hand the range of topics or themes covered by episode tended to be much greater for the

00:09:23.540 --> 00:09:29.200
the HPR shows. The conclusion is, while the delivery tends to be perhaps more flat, what

00:09:29.200 --> 00:09:34.060
is talked about is not just narrowing minded or stuck on one topic, if anything, the reverse

00:09:34.060 --> 00:09:40.040
is true. What's my impression of this whole process? I did get to use a lot of interesting

00:09:40.040 --> 00:09:46.160
tools, predominantly open source libraries available for the Python programming language.

